The Security Council,
Recalling its previous resolutions and statements of its President concerning Mali, in particular resolutions 2374 (2017), 2423 (2018), 2480 (2019), 2541 (2020), 2590 (2021), 2649 (2022) and 2690 (2023),
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, unity, territorial integrity and independence of Mali,
Reaffirming the primary responsibility of the Transition Government of Mali to protect civilians across its territory and to promote and protect human rights and fundamental freedoms in accordance with its international obligations,
Reiterating the centrality of the Agreement on Peace and Reconciliation in Mali emanating from the Algiers process (“the Agreement”) for the achievement of lasting peace in Mali, and calling for its full, effective and accelerated implementation by all parties,
Recalling the commitment of the Transition Government of Mali to achieve the political transition, organize free and fair presidential elections in February 2024, and ensure the return to constitutional order by March 2024, as endorsed by the Economic Community of West African States,
Recalling resolution 2690 (2023) and stressing the importance of the orderly and safe drawdown, withdrawal and liquidation of the United Nations Multidimensional Integrated Stabilization Mission in Mali (MINUSMA), in close consultation with the Transition Government of Mali and in coordination with the troop and police contributing countries, and calling upon the Transition Government of Mali to cooperate fully with the United Nations and to respect all provisions of the Status of Forces Agreement until the departure of the final element of MINUSMA,
Expressing deep concern at the continuing deterioration of the security and humanitarian situation in Mali, including terrorist attacks, intercommunal violence and violations of human rights and international humanitarian law, including reports of massacres, sexual and gender-based violence and grave violations against children, and emphasizing the need to bring those responsible to justice,
Expressing concern at the reports of violations of human rights and international humanitarian law committed by all actors, including the Malian defence and security forces, armed groups, terrorist organizations and foreign private military and security personnel, and recalling the importance of accountability,
Taking note of the request by the Transition Government of Mali to lift the sanctions measures, and reaffirming that sanctions are intended to support peace and stability in Mali and are not intended to have adverse humanitarian consequences for the civilian population or to undermine humanitarian and development assistance,
Recalling the designation criteria set out in resolution 2374 (2017), including for individuals and entities involved in planning, directing or committing acts that violate international human rights law or international humanitarian law, or in obstructing the implementation of the Agreement,
Recalling its resolution 2664 (2022) and reaffirming that the measures renewed by this resolution are not intended to affect humanitarian activities,
Expressing concern at the potential regional impact of the situation in Mali, including the spillover of insecurity and displacement, and emphasizing the need for regional and international cooperation,
Determining that the situation in Mali continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,
Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,
1. Decides to renew until 31 August 2024 the measures imposed by paragraphs 1 to 7 of resolution 2374 (2017), as renewed by resolutions 2423 (2018), 2480 (2019), 2541 (2020), 2590 (2021) and 2649 (2022), and reaffirms that the provisions of paragraphs 8 to 13 of resolution 2374 (2017) shall continue to apply;
2. Reaffirms that the measures set out in resolution 2374 (2017) continue to apply to individuals and entities designated by the Committee established pursuant to resolution 2374 (2017), and calls upon all Member States to implement those measures fully;
3. Decides to extend the mandate of the Panel of Experts established pursuant to resolution 2374 (2017) until 30 September 2024, and requests the Panel to continue to carry out its tasks as set out in resolution 2374 (2017) and to include in its reports information on violations of human rights and international humanitarian law, obstruction of the implementation of the Agreement and the support provided to armed groups and terrorist organizations;
4. Requests the Panel of Experts to provide the Committee with a midterm report by 15 February 2024 and a final report by 15 July 2024, and to submit those reports to the Security Council after consideration by the Committee;
5. Urges all parties and all Member States, as well as international, regional and subregional organizations, to cooperate with the Panel of Experts and to facilitate its work, including by granting unhindered access to persons, documents and sites, and stresses the importance of ensuring the safety of Panel members;
6. Calls upon the Transition Government of Mali to cooperate fully with the United Nations, including the Panel of Experts, and to ensure the safety and security of United Nations personnel, facilities and assets during the drawdown, withdrawal and liquidation of MINUSMA;
7. Reaffirms that the measures renewed by this resolution are not intended to have adverse humanitarian consequences for the civilian population of Mali, and recalls the humanitarian exemptions and authorisation procedures set out in resolution 2374 (2017) and subsequent resolutions, and the applicability of resolution 2664 (2022);
8. Urges the Transition Government of Mali to make progress on the implementation of the Agreement, the fight against impunity, the promotion and protection of human rights, and the return to constitutional order, and affirms its intention to keep the sanctions measures under review, including in light of progress in those areas;
9. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

